Close the O_PATH existence oracle.

  open(p, O_PATH) then readlink("/proc/self/fd/N")

is the loudest of the four "existence" oracles NoMount's per-inode op hijack
cannot answer: on a hidden path it succeeds and hands the caller the full
path back, where an absent path gives -ENOENT. Measured on OP15 (Android 16,
6.12.23) against all 24 blocked uids, with a 7020-path genuinely-absent
control that produced zero false positives.

WHY THE ENGINE CANNOT ANSWER IT

fs/open.c stamps FMODE_PATH before security_file_open() ever runs:

	if (unlikely(f->f_flags & O_PATH)) {
		f->f_mode = FMODE_PATH | FMODE_OPENED;
		...

and path_openat() diverts O_PATH into do_o_path(), which never calls
may_open(). So neither ->open nor ->permission runs, and the hijacked inode
and file operations are never consulted. There is nothing to hook. Verified
against v6.12 fs/open.c and fs/namei.c.

WHERE THE GUARD GOES, AND WHY THERE

Immediately after path_lookupat() returns success, before audit_inode() and
before vfs_open(). That is the earliest point at which a fully resolved,
reference-counted struct path exists.

This is the opposite placement rule from common/_hook/, and the difference is
not an oversight -- read this before moving it. _hook cloaks a STRING (a
SELinux type) in a syscall that reaches the same code either way, so a guard
ahead of the stock permission check answers -EINVAL where a denied caller
would have got -EACCES, and the cloak becomes the detector. That inversion
shipped once and made 5.10/5.15/6.1 more detectable than unpatched.

Here the thing being cloaked is the EXISTENCE OF A PATH COMPONENT, and for a
component that genuinely does not exist the kernel never performs any
file-level permission check at all -- the lookup fails first. So the faithful
emulation is to fail at exactly the point the lookup would have failed, ahead
of every file-level check. The parent directory's search permission is still
enforced, because path_lookupat() has already enforced it: a caller who
cannot search the parent gets -EACCES out of path_lookupat() and never
reaches this guard, exactly as it would for an absent name.

ghost_hidden_path() is false unless the caller's real uid is in the
configured hidden set AND the rendered path matches a configured rule, so a
caller that is not being hidden from is unaffected, and a kernel whose tables
are empty (i.e. one built without the nomount engine's control-plane
forwarder) never changes behaviour at all. See common/_ghost/ghost.c.

APPLIES TO

do_o_path() is byte-identical from v4.19 through torvalds/master, and on all
five pinned OnePlus trees the builder fleet uses -- verified by md5 of the
extracted function body, and on AOSP common android12-5.10 through
android16-6.12.

v4.9 and v4.14 differ only in vfs_open() taking a third current_cred()
argument. The variant that covered them, ghost_o_path_legacy.patch, has been
deleted: nothing in either fleet is older than 5.10, and the builder's
apply_first_of() named ghost_o_path.patch as the required variant on every
kernel version it knows, so the fallback could only ever ABORT the build --
never be chosen. Regenerate it against a real 4.x tree if one ever appears.

--- a/fs/namei.c
+++ b/fs/namei.c
@@ -3901,11 +3901,23 @@ static int do_o_path(struct nameidata *nd, unsigned flags, struct file *file)
 static int do_o_path(struct nameidata *nd, unsigned flags, struct file *file)
 {
 	struct path path;
 	int error = path_lookupat(nd, flags, &path);
+	extern bool ghost_hidden_path(const struct path *gp);
+
 	if (!error) {
+		/*
+		 * O_PATH reaches neither ->open nor ->permission, so a path the
+		 * NoMount engine hides from this uid would otherwise be handed
+		 * straight back through /proc/self/fd/N. Fail where the lookup
+		 * would have failed had the name not existed.
+		 */
+		if (ghost_hidden_path(&path)) {
+			path_put(&path);
+			return -ENOENT;
+		}
 		audit_inode(nd->name, path.dentry, 0);
 		error = vfs_open(&path, file);
 		path_put(&path);
 	}
 	return error;
 }
